      "writtenOn": "2026-11-04T17:30:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It looks like `p3s_vm_iomap_memory()` from aosp/4368009 open-codes the same 4KB bound:\n\n```\n\taligned_len \u003d PAGE_ALIGN_4KB(len);\n\tif (offset \u003e aligned_len || vm_len \u003e aligned_len - offset)\n\t\treturn -EINVAL;\n```\n\nWould it make sense for it to call this helper instead, so that both 4KB bounds share one definition? Something like:\n\n```\n\tif (start + len \u003c start || vma_exceeds_object(vma, len))\n\t\treturn -EINVAL;\n```\n\nAs far as I can see the two checks agree for every VMA, since `vm_len` is never 0. That would mean adding the helper in aosp/4368009, or switching `p3s_vm_iomap_memory()` over in this patch, whichever you prefer.\n\nThe comment could then describe the check rather than the DMA caller, e.g.:\n\n```\n/*\n * vma_exceeds_object - Check if a 4KB VMA maps past the end of an object\n * @vma: Pointer to struct vm_area_struct\n * @size: Size of the object in bytes\n *\n * A 4KB VMA may map from vma_file_offset() up to @size rounded up to 4KB,\n * but not the padding after it in the last kernel page. Always false for\n * native VMAs, whose callers keep their kernel page bounds.\n */\n```",
